     "question": "Explain why the ionic radii of Li+ and Mg2+ are similar, and why the thermal decomposition of lithium nitrate is similar to that of magnesium nitrate but different from sodium nitrate. Include equations.",
     "status": "ready",
     "answer": "Li+ and Mg2+ are similar in size (diagonal relationship: Mg2+ has a greater nuclear charge but also more charge); Li and Mg nitrates decompose to oxide, NO2 and O2, while NaNO3 gives nitrite and O2",
     "steps": [
      "Li+ and Mg2+ are isoelectronic (1s2 configuration) and have similar sizes; Mg2+ has a larger nuclear charge that pulls its electrons closer, offsetting its extra positive charge.",
      "So Li+ and Mg2+ have similar charge densities, which explains their similar chemical behaviour (diagonal relationship).",
      "Lithium nitrate decomposes: 4LiNO3 -> 2Li2O + 4NO2 + O2, like magnesium nitrate: 2Mg(NO3)2 -> 2MgO + 4NO2 + O2.",
      "Both Li+ and Mg2+ have high charge density, so they polarise the nitrate ion strongly, weakening N-O bonds and forming oxides.",
      "Sodium nitrate has a larger, lower charge density Na+, which polarises the nitrate less: 2NaNO3 -> 2NaNO2 + O2.",
      "So lithium and magnesium nitrates give NO2 and an oxide, while sodium nitrate gives nitrite and oxygen only."
     ],
