     "no": "Q40(a)",
     "marks": 6,
     "topic": "Mechanisms From Rate Equations",
     "keywords": [
      "1-bromobutane",
      "2-bromo-2-methylpropane",
      "rate equation",
      "SN2",
      "SN1",
      "hydroxide",
      "mechanism"
     ],
     "question": "Explain how the rate equations rate = k[CH3CH2CH2CH2Br][OH-] and rate = k[(CH3)3CBr] show the different mechanisms for reaction of hydroxide ions with these halogenoalkanes.",
     "status": "ready",
     "answer": "1-bromobutane: bimolecular SN2 with OH- in the rate-determining step; 2-bromo-2-methylpropane: unimolecular SN1 via a carbocation",
     "steps": [
      "1-bromobutane: rate = k[RX][OH-] is first order in each, so second order overall.",
      "Both RX and OH- are in the rate-determining step, so the nucleophile attacks as the halide leaves (SN2, one concerted step).",
      "2-bromo-2-methylpropane: rate = k[(CH3)3CBr] depends only on the halogenoalkane.",
      "The C-Br bond breaks in the slow step to form a tertiary carbocation, which is then attacked by OH- (SN1).",
      "The 2-bromo-2-methylpropane mechanism is therefore unimolecular in the rate-determining step.",
      "The rate equations show the rate-determining step involves OH- for the primary case but not for the tertiary case."
     ],
