     "question": "Compare and contrast the mechanism of platinum as a catalyst in car exhaust treatment with that of vanadium(V) oxide in the Contact Process (general catalyst definitions not required).",
     "status": "ready",
     "answer": "Both are heterogeneous. Pt adsorbs gases on its surface without changing its oxidation state; V2O5 is reduced and reoxidised, so V changes oxidation state.",
     "steps": [
      "Both are solid catalysts, so both are heterogeneous and provide a surface.",
      "Pt: CO and NO are adsorbed onto the surface, weakening bonds; adjacent molecules react and products desorb.",
      "Pt keeps its oxidation state, unchanged, during the reaction.",
      "V2O5: SO2 reduces V(V) to V(IV), e.g. SO2 + V2O5 -> SO3 + 2VO2.",
      "V(IV) is reoxidised by O2: 2VO2 + 1/2 O2 -> V2O5, so V cycles between +4 and +5.",
      "Contrast: the Pt mechanism uses surface adsorption; the V2O5 mechanism uses a redox cycle with the solid oxide."
     ],
