     "question": "Explain why butylamine is a stronger base than ammonia.",
     "status": "ready",
     "answer": "The butyl group is electron-donating (positive inductive effect), raising the electron density on N, so its lone pair accepts H+ more readily.",
     "steps": [
      "The alkyl chain of butylamine pushes electron density towards the N atom (positive inductive effect).",
      "This makes the N lone pair more available to accept a proton.",
      "The butylammonium ion is a weaker acid than NH4+, since the alkyl group stabilises the positive charge less favourably for losing H+.",
      "So the equilibrium C4H9NH2 + H2O to C4H9NH3+ + OH- lies further to the right than for NH3: butylamine is the stronger base."
     ],
